      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Post%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "George Washington erected the original white post to guide travelers to Fairfax's estate.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Post%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The intersection that gave White Post its name hosted Meade Memorial Church, a Methodist church, and a Masonic Lodge by the late 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Post%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Several historic sites, including Greenway Court, Bethel Memorial Church, and the White Post Historic District, are on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Post%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "White Post’s heritage reflects German and Scots‑Irish subsistence farmers and prominent families like the Meades, Randolphs, and Burwells.",
